schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Community momentum flywheel and rhythm model"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A self-reinforcing cycle (ship → celebrate → invite → onboard → empower
    → contribute → acknowledge → repeat) paired with predictable rhythms at
    weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual cadences, that keeps a local
    node's energy from fading after its initial launch excitement.
  source_traditions:
    - Greenpill Local Regen Guide (cited generically in the article's frontmatter sources)
    - general community-building and DAO-governance literature (cited generically, not by specific title)
    - East African community traditions the article names as existing precedent (saving circles, inter-clan coordination, post-jumu'ah community meals)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Framed as a structural fix for the observation that "many nodes launch
    with enthusiasm, then fade... the culprit is rarely a lack of care, it's
    a lack of structure." The article explicitly asks organizers to channel
    existing community rhythms into regen finance work "in ways that feel
    natural, not foreign," rather than importing an unfamiliar cadence.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - generic "community engagement" growth-hacking frameworks (funnel-style, not necessarily rooted in local relational practice)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      the article treats "momentum" as distinct from "activity": momentum is
      meaningful, self-reinforcing activity, not just frequency of events.
    - >-
      "dips" (attendance drops, energy decreases) are explicitly named as
      normal and often seasonal (harvest season, exams, Ramadan), not a sign
      of failure — a distinction the article asks organizers to plan for
      rather than panic over.
  tensions:
    - >-
      the article's own claim that this rhythm model builds on "existing
      traditions rather than inventing new ones" is asserted at a general
      "East African contexts" level rather than sourced to a specific named
      community or community-authored account.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating this as a generic, portable "growth loop" framework risks
    stripping out the article's own framing that the model is meant to
    channel practices (saving circles, communal meals, rotational
    leadership) that specific communities already hold — presenting it as
    culturally neutral would misrepresent the source.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/building-momentum.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-88bafbd86463
  source_lineage: building-momentum.md
